{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/python","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:11.116Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/python/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/python/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Python image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Python?\n\nPython is an interpreted, interactive, object-oriented, open-source programming language. It incorporates modules, exceptions, dynamic typing, very high-level dynamic data types, and classes. Python combines remarkable power with very clear syntax. It has interfaces to many system calls and libraries, as well as to various window systems, and is extensible in C or C++. It is also usable as an extension language for applications that need a programmable interface. Finally, Python is portable: it runs on many Unix variants, on the Mac, and on Windows 2000 and later.\n\nSee: [Python (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_%28programming_language%29)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `3`\n- `3-cm2.0`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of the image. Additional tags that are specific to python version, Mariner build, or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running a simple Python app\n\n`1.` Create a Dockerfile to generate a new image that includes the necessary content.\n\n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/python:3\n\n    # Copy files and install app dependencies.\n    COPY requirements.txt ./\n    RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n    COPY . .\n\n    # Start the app.\n    CMD [ \"python\", \"./your-daemon-or-script.py\" ]\n\n`2.` Build and run your container image.\n\n    docker build -t python-app-image .\n    docker run -it --rm --name running-python-app python-app-image\n\nThe default container startup command is `python3`.\n\n### Run a single Python script\n\nFor many simple, single file projects, you may find it inconvenient to write a complete Dockerfile. In such cases, you can run a Python script (named my-script.py in the example below) by using the Mariner based Python docker image directly:\n    \n    docker run -it --rm --name running-python-script -v \"$PWD\":/usr/src/app -w /usr/src/app mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/python:3 python3 my-script.py\n\n### Run Python repl environment\n\nYou may want to simply run the interactive shell that comes with Python by launching it when the container starts. In such case, you can run Python by using the Mariner based Python docker image directly. The following command will launch the virtual environment that Python comes with:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/python:3\n\n### Launch Mariner shell\n\nThe Python image ships with a bash shell. The launch command can be provided to docker run command to launch Mariner shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/python:3 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T16:52:28.1569396+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"548K+","stars":null},"mediaTypes":[],"contentTypes":[],"storage":null,"tags":[{"name":"3.9-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:d1dd9c1296ec1a311efc2dd35d74a9c8f2be3c5e264d9a26c847b1123807312c","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:20:25.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3.9","digest":"sha256:d1dd9c1296ec1a311efc2dd35d74a9c8f2be3c5e264d9a26c847b1123807312c","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:20:15.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:d1dd9c1296ec1a311efc2dd35d74a9c8f2be3c5e264d9a26c847b1123807312c","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:20:05.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3","digest":"sha256:d1dd9c1296ec1a311efc2dd35d74a9c8f2be3c5e264d9a26c847b1123807312c","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:19:58.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3.